<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Achaemenid Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Achaemenids, first Persian Empire, Achaemenid dynasty, Achaemenid Persian Empire, Achaemenid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Achaemenid Empire was the first Persian Empire, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> in the sixth century BCE and stretching at its height from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>. It governed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapies</a>, royal roads and a policy of tolerating local customs and religions. It absorbed the Median, Babylonian and Egyptian realms and was conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> in the 330s BCE, influencing later states such as the Seleucid and Sasanian empires.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire.jpg" alt="Achaemenid Empire" width="320" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cattette · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Achaemenid_Empire_500_BCE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The empire takes its name from Achaemenes, credited as the progenitor of the dynasty, a minor seventh-century ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anshan">Anshan</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>. By the 7th century BCE the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persis">Persis</a> had been settled by Persian people. From Persis Cyrus rose. He revolted against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/median-empire">Median Empire</a> in 553 BCE and in 550 BCE defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a>, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astyages">Astyages</a> and took the Median capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecbatana">Ecbatana</a>. By inheriting Astyages' empire he also inherited its conflicts with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Neo-Babylonian Empire</a>, both of which he went on to defeat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Cyrus's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a> used the army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-iii">Psamtik III</a>, while Cyrus himself died fighting a local Iranian insurgency. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> reinforced the empire, expanded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a> as a ceremonial capital and established the first Persian royal navy. The empire was repulsed from Scythia in 513 BCE and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in 492–490 BCE and 480–479 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-revolt">Ionian Revolt</a> of 499 to 493 BCE began when Aristagoras, tyrant of Miletus, incited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> against Darius.</p><p>In 343 BCE Artaxerxes III invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> with 330,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> and 14,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>. Artaxerxes IV Arses was poisoned by the eunuch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagoas">Bagoas</a>, who set <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a> on the throne. In 334 BCE Alexander the Great invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, and in 330 BCE the empire was conquered in its entirety. Later Persian rulers in Fārs, the Frataraka, acted for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> and then became independent kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persis">Persis</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Cyrus founded the empire as a multi-state realm governed from four capitals: Pasargadae, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecbatana">Ecbatana</a>. It was divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapies</a>, between 20 and 30 at different times, each with a satrap, a general and a state secretary. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daric">daric</a>, a gold coin, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> siglos, and a tax system tailored to each satrapy: Babylon was assessed at 1,000 silver <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> supplied 120,000 measures of grain and 700 talents of silver.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immortals">Immortals</a> were a unit of 10,000 trained soldiers, and a postal system ran through relay stations. Elamite was the language of the chancellery while the court sat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> gained in importance under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-i">Artaxerxes I</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> reports that Cyrus released the Jewish people from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-captivity">Babylonian captivity</a> and helped rebuild the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Temple in Jerusalem</a>. Four kings are buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-rostam">Naqsh-e Rostam</a>, and Cyrus's tomb at Pasargadae is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Achaemenid Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Third Dynasty of Ur (c. 2112–2004 BCE), the Old Babylonian kingdom, the Assyrian Empire and in the end the <b>Achaemenid Empire</b> all drew on administrative, ideological and cultural precedents set in the Akkadian period, and later Mesopotamian kings revered Sargon and Naram-Sin as models of kingship.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It passed under the Chaldean Empire and from 539 BCE under the <b>Achaemenid Empire</b>, when it minted its own silver coins showing warships and was home to a Persian-period fortress.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After Cyrus the Great conquered Lydia in 546 BCE, Chios, according to Herodotus, submitted to the <b>Achaemenid Empire</b> of its own accord, although the Persians as yet had no fleet.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Greco-Persian Wars began with the conquest of the Greek cities of Ionia by the <b>Achaemenid Persian Empire</b> of Cyrus the Great shortly after 550 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delian-league">Delian League · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When Cyrus the Great took Babylon in 539 BCE Dilmun passed to the <b>Achaemenid Empire</b>, and it disappears from the record as a political entity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dilmun">Dilmun · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Cyrus the Great absorbed Elam into the <b>Achaemenid Empire</b> in the 540s and 530s BCE and ended its political independence, yet Darius I soon made Susa a principal Persian royal capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
